+# forestsearch 0.3.1
+
+## The consistency screen no longer treats `plan = "sequential"` as a parallel configuration
+
+`subgroup.consistency()` validated `parallel_args` against `c("plan", "workers")`
+and, when both were present, took the future-backed batching path even for
+`plan = "sequential"` or `workers = 1`: the candidates were split into
+`future_lapply()` calls of `min(2 * workers, n)` -- two at a time on one
+worker -- and every call paid a full round of globals resolution around an
+evaluation costing a few milliseconds.  `forestsearch_bootstrap_dofuture()`
+forces exactly that configuration on the inner search of every replicate.
+Measured on the profiled fixture (`REPORT_bootstrap_profile_2026-08-30.md`):
+29.4 s per call, 86% of it in `future_lapply` globals resolution, against
+4.5 s for the identical search under the plain loop.
+
+1. **Dispatch.**  A sequential plan, or `workers <= 1` under any plan, now runs
+   the consistency screen as the plain in-process loop.  No future backend is
+   set up and no warning is emitted -- the code is doing what the caller asked.
+   The genuinely parallel path (`multisession` / `multicore` / `callr` with
+   `workers >= 2`) and the batch-size logic are untouched;
+   `parallel_args$batch_size` remains the knob for per-batch overhead on
+   parallel plans.
+2. **Measured effect.**  Continuous MD fixture (n = 500, 749 candidates
+   screened): 31.1 s -> 5.5 s per `forestsearch()` call (5.6x).  Survival gbsg
+   fixture (120 candidates screened): 8.9 s -> 7.5 s.  A 20-replicate
+   `forestsearch_bootstrap_dofuture()` on the continuous fixture: 32.2 s ->
+   4.97 s per replicate; B = 1000 projects to 83 min sequential
+   against 490-537 min before.
+3. **Results under `consistency_method = "resample"` are unchanged** -- the
+   closed form consumes no RNG, and five reference configurations (sequential
+   with and without early stopping, survival, and a two-worker multisession
+   control) are `identical()` before and after on the selected subgroup, its
+   membership, the candidate table, the consistency counters and the fitted
+   effect.
+4. **`consistency_method = "split"` users:** the plain loop seeds once
+   (`set.seed(seed)`) where the backend assigned one L'Ecuyer stream per
+   candidate, so the random splits are drawn in a different order.  Each
+   candidate's consistency rate moves within its own Monte-Carlo noise at the
+   chosen `fs.splits` (measured at 50 splits: sd of the per-candidate
+   difference 0.028 against a binomial sd of 0.042; the selected subgroup
+   unchanged).  Results are not bit-identical to 0.3.0 on that path; they are
+   equally valid draws.
+5. The driver-style `parallel_args = list(plan = "sequential")` (no `workers`)
+   now reaches the plain loop through this rule rather than by tripping the
+   `"parallel_args missing required elements. Using sequential."` warning,
+   which no longer fires for that input.
+
